 "notes": [
  "DWSIM molar enthalpy and entropy exclude the formation terms: thermo-sim minus DWSIM is a constant per component and phase (benzene H +82.86 kJ/mol, S -157.2 kJ/kmol/K; toluene H +50.03 kJ/mol, S -242.1 kJ/kmol/K), so the tests compare differences.",
  "Vapor: the benzene offset is constant to 4 J/mol over 100 K (Cp ideal gas agrees to 0.3 %); toluene vapor drifts 10 J/mol over 16 K (Cp 0.35 % below DWSIM, 1.527 against 1.5325 kJ/kg/K at 400 K). Phase change: the offset is the same on both sides, so the heat of vaporization agrees (the difference across the change is below 2 J/mol of a 30 kJ/mol latent heat).",
  "Liquid: the heat capacity implied by the DWSIM enthalpy differences is 0.4 to 0.8 % above thermo-sim, so the liquid enthalpy difference between 300 K and the boiling point is 0.5 % larger in DWSIM (benzene 36.7 J/mol in 52 K, toluene 64.3 J/mol in 79 K). CAUSE IDENTIFIED: the ideal gas heat capacity. Integrating the DWSIM polynomial (ideal_gas_cp_dwsim) against the thermo-sim one (database polynomial) gives 36.9 J/mol (benzene, 300 to 352.6 K) and 64.4 J/mol (toluene, 300 to 378.9 K), the same differences. The DWSIM ideal gas Cp is 1.2 % (benzene) and 0.8 % (toluene) above thermo-sim at 300 K and 0.3 % below / 0.4 % above at 400 K. The departure (EOS residual) terms agree. The vapor differences are small because the two polynomials cross near 370 K (benzene).",
  "The 'Heat Capacity (Liquid)' column printed by DWSIM (1.448 kJ/kg/K for benzene at 300 K) is 6 % below dH/dT of its own enthalpy (120.3 J/mol/K = 1.54 kJ/kg/K), so it is not a consistent reference for the liquid Cp: use the enthalpy differences.",
  "Density: liquid 0.006 %, vapor 0.01 %."
 ],
